  "liner": {
   "body_md": "This recording captures the Mountain Goats performing at the Capitol Theater in Olympia, Washington, on Thursday, July 19, 2001 [1]. The uploader describes the event as the Yo-Yo-A-Go-Go festival, which took place in July of 2001 [1]. The audio file, labeled tmg2001-07-19, is the only available recording for this specific date and contains twelve songs with a total runtime of thirty-seven minutes [1]. It is the twenty-ninth show in the archive’s collection for the band, following a previous archived performance at the Bottom of the Hill on June 23, 2001 [2]. The setlist includes “The Plague,” which is the first in the archive and has only eight archived performances [1][2]. “Jaipur” is also the first in the archive [1][2]. “Fault Lines” is the first in the archive and is rare, with eight archived performances [1][2]. “Shower” is rare, with seven archived performances [1][2]. “Downtown Seoul” is a bustout, appearing for the first time in twenty-five archived shows after 2,280 days, and is rare with twelve archived performances [1][2]. “Transjordanian Blues” is the first in the archive and rare, with twelve archived performances [1][2]. Other tracks include “Minnesota,” “There Will Be No Divorce,” “Song for an Old Friend,” “Alpha Omega,” “The Best Ever Death Metal Band in Denton,” and “Going to Georgia” [1].",
